  "response": "# Hotspot triage plan for 04-monorepo\n\n**Two caveats about the data:**\n- `churn` and `recency` are 0 on every finding, so the scan has no change-history signal. The order below is based only on the import graph.\n- Several `large_function` findings sit inside each other. For example, in `src/index.ts`, `build` (167–459) contains `<anonymous>` (183–456), which contains `mainTasks` (260–453), which contains `buildAll` (291–365) and `startWatcher` (367–445). That is one large tangle reported five times, so I count by tangle, not by finding.\n\n## Hotspots by connectivity\n\n| File | Fan-in | Fan-out | Transitive importers | Size and length findings | Test gap |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `src/utils.ts` | **18** (p99 = 10) | 1 | **26** | 424-line file | 0 |\n| `src/index.ts` | 4 | **17** (p99 = 11) | 4 | 459-line file; `build` 293 lines, `mainTasks` 194, `normalizeOptions` 96 | 0 |\n| `src/esbuild/index.ts` | 1 | **11** (= p99) | 4 | `runEsbuild` 237 lines, 38 option reads | 0 |\n| `src/plugin.ts` | 10 | 3 | 16 | `buildFinished` 103 lines | 0.75 |\n| `src/log.ts` | 9 | 0 | 30 | none | 0.75 |\n| `test/utils.ts` | 9 | 0 | 9 | none | 0 |\n| `src/rollup.ts` | 0 | 7 | 0 | `getRollupConfig` 122 lines | 0.75 |\n| `src/api-extractor.ts` | 1 | 6 (= p95) | 4 | `rollupDtsFiles` 85 lines | 0.75 |\n\n## Triage order\n\n### Step 0 (before any refactor): fix the tests that guard `src/utils.ts`\nThe scan links 12 weak or empty tests to `src/utils.ts` as their code under test. These include crime_00008, which has no assertions, and crime_00016, 17, 29, 30, 33, 34, 41, 42, 54, 63 and 68, which use only weak matchers. On paper `test_gap = 0` for `utils.ts`, but the tests covering it prove very little. Splitting a module with 18 importers behind those tests is not safe. Strengthen them first; each is small effort.\n\n### 1. `src/utils.ts`: split it first, because it has the largest blast radius\n- **Evidence:** fan-in 18 (about 2× the p95 cutoff of 9), 26 transitive importers, the largest in the repo apart from `log.ts`. It is also a 424-line file (1.4× the threshold).\n- **Why first:** every edit to this file currently reaches 18 direct importers. Splitting it by responsibility means each importer depends only on the part it uses, so later edits reach fewer files. No other refactor here narrows the reach of future changes as much.\n- **How:** split it into cohesive modules and keep `utils.ts` as a re-export barrel at first, so none of the 18 importers changes in the same commit. Then move importers over gradually.\n- **While in there:** `jsoncParse` (crime_00032) catches errors and returns `{}`, which makes a parse failure look like empty config to all 26 transitive importers. Make it throw, or return a typed result the caller can check.\n\n### 2. `src/index.ts`: break up the orchestrator\n- **Evidence:** fan-out 17, the highest in the repo and well above p99 (11). It is a 459-line file containing the 293-line `build` tangle, plus `normalizeOptions` at 96 lines. `build` and `buildAll` also hide `removeFiles` side effects behind names that sound pure (crime_00009, crime_00024).\n- **Why second:** its outward blast radius is small (4 importers), so restructuring it is cheap and fairly safe. The problem runs the other way: with 17 imports, a change in any of them can break it. That makes it the hardest file in the repo for an agent to edit correctly.\n- **How:** move `normalizeOptions`, `buildAll` and `startWatcher` into their own modules and keep `build` as a thin coordinator. The existing tests (`test_gap 0`) cover the public `build` entry point.\n\n### 3. `src/esbuild/index.ts`: split `runEsbuild`\n- **Evidence:** fan-out 11 (= p99), and `runEsbuild` is 237 lines (4× the threshold). Its options parameter is read in 38 different places.\n- **Why third:** it has the same shape as `index.ts`, smaller. It has one importer and 4 transitive importers, so the risk is contained. Give it a typed options record, then pull out the plugin assembly and the output handling as separate helpers.\n\n### 4. `src/plugin.ts`: refactor only `buildFinished`, not the module structure\n- **Evidence:** fan-in 10, but **9 of those 10 importers use types only**. As the scan says, a shared type module's coupling is compile-time, and being depended on is its job. The real issue is `buildFinished`, a 103-line class method with 16 transitive importers and `test_gap 0.75`.\n- **How:** write characterisation tests first, then pull helpers out of the method. Leave the import graph as it is.\n\n## Leave alone, or only touch locally\n\n- **`src/log.ts`** (fan-in 9, fan-out 0, 4 importers use types only): a logger with no dependencies of its own that many files import. Its high transitive reach (30) shows it is load-bearing, not that its structure is wrong. Don't restructure it.\n- **`test/utils.ts`** (fan-in 9, fan-out 0): a shared test helper, so being imported by many tests is expected. At most, bound the `Promise.all` loop that writes files (crime_00023).\n- **`src/rollup.ts`** (fan-out 7, **fan-in 0, blast radius 0**): `getRollupConfig` is 122 lines and untested. Nothing imports it, though, so refactoring it does nothing to reduce blast radius. Rename it to reveal its side effects (`setSilent`, `removeFiles`, crime_00002) and wait until it has tests before doing more.\n- **`src/api-extractor.ts`** (fan-out 6, exactly at p95; fan-in 1): borderline. Only fix the swallowed error (crime_00020: pass `err` to the logger).\n- **Plugin leaves** (`esbuild/postcss.ts` 140/126 lines, `esbuild/svelte.ts` 116 lines, `rollup/ts-resolve.ts` 88 lines, `cli-main.ts` `main` 153 lines): each has 1–2 direct importers. These are local cleanups that can happen whenever someone works on those files. They won't reduce blast radius across the codebase.\n\n## Summary\nStrengthen the tests first, then split `utils.ts` (fan-in 18), then break up `index.ts` (fan-out 17), then `esbuild/index.ts` (fan-out 11). For `plugin.ts`, work only inside `buildFinished`. Leave `log.ts` and `test/utils.ts` alone: they are widely imported because that is their job.\n\nSince the scan has no churn data, re-run it with git history before committing to this order. A busy file with less coupling might deserve to move up.",
